<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Soshangane</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1780–<a href="/years/1858">1858</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Manukosi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Soshangane was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndwandwe">Ndwandwe</a> commander who fled north after Shaka's victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwide">Zwide</a> around 1819 and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaza-empire">Gaza Empire</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>. Conquering Tsonga and Ndau communities and raiding Portuguese settlements, he built a kingdom stretching from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limpopo">Limpopo</a> towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a>. His followers became known as Shangaan after him.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Soshangane, born Soshangana Nxumalo, was born around 1780 in present-day KwaNongoma, the son of Zikode kaGasa, a chief of the junior branch of the Nxumalo clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndwandwe">Ndwandwe</a> people. His family lived in the Mkhuze region near eTshanini Mountain, while the senior branch under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwide">Zwide</a> lived at Magudu near the Pongola valley. Under Zwide the Ndwandwe confederacy grew by absorbing smaller communities and by turning age groups formed at state-wide initiation rites into military regiments. Soshangane was one of Zwide's generals, beside Mzilikazi Khumalo, Nxaba Msene and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwangendaba">Zwangendaba</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Fleeing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> king with his brothers and his people, he moved along the eastern foothills of the Lubombo to the upper Ntembe River, where Captain W. Owen met them in 1822, a meeting described as the first recorded encounter between the English and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguni">Nguni</a>. For about five years he grew strong by raiding in the Tembe area, defeating the Ronga clans around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delagoa-bay">Delagoa Bay</a>, taking their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and young women and drafting their young men into his army. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndwandwe">Ndwandwe</a> refugees joined him after Shaka defeated Sikhunyani in 1826, and in 1828 he defeated Shaka's army.</p><p>Around 1825 he entered the country between the Matsolo and Nkomati rivers, where a brief alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwangendaba">Zwangendaba</a> broke down over his ambition to found his own kingdom. In 1835 he drove out Nxaba Msene, who had ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofala">Sofala</a> for about ten years, and he then conquered the Ndau and the Manyika.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Soshangane gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> to his sons and close relatives, who heard subjects' problems before they reached the king, so that his elder sons gained political experience and followers. Regiments called mangas or impis, commanded by indunas and modelled on Shaka's, dominated the local peasantry; some twenty-four age sets of regiments were formed while the state lasted. Intermarriage with the Rhonga, Tonga and Chopi diluted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguni">Nguni</a>'s distinctiveness, and the rulers required subjects to adopt customs such as pierced earlobes. He ruled until his death in 1858.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Soshangane” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom’s rise reshaped southern Africa: groups fleeing Zulu expansion or breaking away from it founded new states, among them the Ndebele Kingdom of Mzilikazi, while the Gaza kingdom of <b>Soshangane</b> emerged from the defeated Ndwandwe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zulu-kingdom">Zulu Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Soshangane</b>, a commander of Zwide's Ndwandwe Kingdom, fled north after the Ndwandwe defeat by the Zulu and by about 1824 had settled in southern Mozambique.</blockquote><a href="/state/gaza-empire">Gaza Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Ndwandwe commanders <b>Soshangane</b> and Zwangendaba founded the Gaza state and the Ngoni kingdoms far to the north.</blockquote><a href="/state/ndwandwe-kingdom">Ndwandwe Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
